Site: crate-jmap-chat-server/src/emoji.rs:481-525 (update) and 595-639 (destroy).

The variable  encodes:
- outer Option: did the pre-fetch find this emoji?
- inner Option: does the (found) emoji have a spaceId?

Reading the call site requires holding both layers in mind:
    let existing_space_id: Option<Option<Id>> = match backend.get_objects(...).await {
        Ok((found, _)) => found.first().map(|emoji| emoji.space_id.clone()),
        Err(e) => { ...serverFail and continue }
    };
    if let Some(scope) = existing_space_id.as_ref() {
        let scope_ref: Option<&Id> = scope.as_ref();
        ...

Suggested rewrite: collapse the outer Option into an early continue:

    let Ok((found, _)) = backend.get_objects(...).await else {
        ...serverFail and continue
    };
    let Some(emoji) = found.first() else {
        // Emoji not found; skip the gate and let update_object surface notFound.
        match backend.update_object(...).await { ... };
        continue;
    };
    let scope_ref: Option<&Id> = emoji.space_id.as_ref();
    match backend.may_set_custom_emoji(caller, &account_id, scope_ref, EmojiSetOp::Update).await { ... }
    ...

The early continue removes one Option layer; the remaining Option<&Id> is meaningful (Some=Space-scoped, None=global). The control flow becomes linear instead of nested.

This is a readability nit (P4), not a correctness issue. The current code works.
